# klip-dictionary-data v4 — provenance manifest
#
# Every number here is measured, not carried over. One block per language.
#
# **v4 REBUILDS ALL NINETEEN LANGUAGES.** Unlike v3, which rebuilt three and
# carried sixteen, every block below is REBUILT at the builder commit named
# next -- because the entry classifier changed in a way that can move rows in
# any language, and it did in all nineteen.
#
# BUILDER      Klip commit 1f61855a8e73a9389620929c909afa11eead545f (develop,
#              the #258 merge), tool/dict_pack/build_pack.dart
# EXPORTER     export_pack.py in this repo, python3 -I, stdlib only.
#              A column-to-key dump of the pack. No filtering, no reshaping.
#              UNCHANGED from v2/v3 and re-attached byte-identical.
# CHAIN        kaikki extract -> Dart pack (.sqlite) -> gzipped JSONL asset
#
# WHAT CHANGED: an entry's CLASS was decided by a union over every sense's
# tags, so one "short for ..." sub-sense demoted the whole word -- `Wasser`
# shipped as an abbreviation of `Mineralwasser`. Class is now read from the
# entry's own tags, else its FIRST GLOSSED SENSE. Apocopic forms resolve to
# `form`. See the README's v4 changelog.
#
# ALL NINETEEN EXTRACTS WERE RE-VERIFIED BEFORE THE BUILD, not assumed: each
# extract.sha256 below was recomputed from the file in ~/dict-work/ and
# matched the value v3 recorded (v2's, for the sixteen v3 carried). Every
# input is therefore pinned across v2, v3 and v4, so the class deltas are
# attributable to the classifier change alone and to nothing in the source.
#
# ── pack.sha256 IS NOT RECORDED IN v4, AND THAT IS A REGRESSION ──────────
#
# v2 and v3 recorded a sha256 for each intermediate .sqlite pack. v4 does
# NOT: each pack was deleted immediately after its export and WAS NOT HASHED
# FIRST. The honest entry is "NOT CAPTURED" -- v3's hashes are NOT carried
# forward here, because a different builder produces a different pack and
# copying them would be a false provenance record.
#
# WHAT IS STILL VERIFIABLE END TO END: extract.sha256 pins the input, the
# builder commit pins the code, and asset.sha256 pins the PUBLISHED artifact.
# A rebuild from the pinned extract at the pinned commit, re-exported, must
# reproduce the asset hash -- which is the thing consumers actually fetch.
# The pack hash was a convenience on an intermediate that is not published.
#
# FOR THE NEXT RELEASE: hash the pack at export time, before deleting it.
#
# THE PACKS ARE INTERMEDIATES AND WERE DELETED AFTER EXPORT. They are not
# published. pack.bytes is recorded, read from each builder's own output.
#

